<br/>
The latest &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; changes the balance between these two devices. The &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; retains the core design introduced in 2020, while the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; has just received a substantial redesign with an &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display, a thinner and lighter enclosure, and several features unavailable on the Air. With the mini also costing &#36;50 less, the current &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; is harder to recommend unless you specifically need its larger workspace and keyboard support and cannot wait for its successor.<br/>
<br/>
For most buyers looking for a handheld tablet to complement a laptop or desktop computer, the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; is now the more compelling choice. Its portability advantage is substantial: the Wi-Fi model weighs 255 grams, compared with 464 grams for the 11-inch Air, and its smaller footprint makes it much easier to hold and carry. Combined with its &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display, water and dust resistance, and fast charging, this makes it particularly well suited to reading, watching videos, playing handheld games, and taking notes on the go.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; remains the better choice for productivity. Even the 11-inch model provides considerably more screen space for documents, drawing, and working across multiple apps. Support for Apple's Magic Keyboard makes the &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; a much more practical potential laptop replacement. The &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; has ample processing power for many of the same tasks and supports Bluetooth keyboards, but its smaller display and lack of an equivalent integrated keyboard accessory make it less suitable as a productivity device.<br/>
<br/>
These are meaningful reasons to choose the &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; if you need a tablet immediately. The 11-inch model's &#36;50 premium buys a larger workspace and keyboard support, although the Magic Keyboard costs extra and whether the combination can replace your laptop depends on your apps and workflows being supported by iPadOS. Its older design does not diminish that clear utility, but buying one now means accepting an LCD display and missing several of the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj;'s newer features.<br/>
<br/>
The 13-inch &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; provides the clearest distinction from the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj;, with substantially more room for side-by-side documents, drawing, and multitasking. Its &#36;250 premium over the base &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; is worth considering if you regularly need that added display area, although its larger dimensions and weight make it much less convenient for handheld use. The 11-inch Air offers a more portable balance for switching between tablet and keyboard use, but neither approaches the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj;'s ease of carrying and holding.<br/>
<br/>
For prospective &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; buyers who do not need a new device immediately, we recommend waiting. A new &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; with an &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/01/three-new-ipads-in-first-half-of-2027/">expected in the first half of 2027</a>. This would address one of the current &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj;'s most significant disadvantages compared with the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj;, likely along with other new features to bring it up to par. With the &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; newly refreshed and a consequential &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; update expected in the not-too-distant future, postponing a nonessential &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; purchase is the more sensible choice.<div class="linkback">Related Roundups: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-air/">iPad Air </a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-mini/">iPad mini</a></div><div class="linkback">Buyer's Guide: <a href="https://buyersguide.macrumors.com/#iPad-Air">iPad Air (Neutral)</a>, <a href="https://buyersguide.macrumors.com/#iPad_Mini">iPad Mini (Buy Now)</a></div><div class="linkback">Related Forum: <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/forums/ipad.137">iPad</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/ipad-mini-vs-ipad-air-2026/">iPad Mini vs. iPad Air Buyer&#039;s Guide: Now Just &#36;50 Apart</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/ipad-mini-vs-ipad-air-buyers-guide-now-just-50-apart.2493756/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>